          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This entry discusses
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-representations"} ∞-representations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-infinity+algebroids"} Lie-infinity algebroids
            (generalizing
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+actions"} Lie algebra actions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
          %p
            Recall that an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} algebroid
            is both a
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            as well as a
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of a Lie algebra: it is
          %p
            to Lie algebras as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} Lie ∞-groupoids
            are to Lie groups.
          %p
            Accordingly, the notion of
            %em
              representation of a Lie-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-algebroid
            is a horizontal and vertical categorification of the ordinary notion of representation of a Lie algebra, which in turn is the linearization of the notion of representation of a Lie group.
          %p
            In view of this notice that there are essentially two fundamental ways to express the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gr
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                as a morphism out of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Gr
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %li
              %p
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibration sequence
                over
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Gr
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
          %p
            While essentially equivalent, it is noteworthy that the first definition naturally takes place in the context of not-necessarily smooth (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-)categories, while the second one usually remains within the context of smooth (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            )-groupoids:
          %p
            namely for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a Lie group, for definiteness and for simplicity, with corresponding one-object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} G
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            –, a linear representation in terms of an action morphisms is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho : \mathbf{B} G \to Vect
          %p
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} G
            to the category of vector spaces. In fact, there is a canonical equivalence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{B}G, Vect]
            with the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep(G)
            of linear representations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\mathbf{B}G, Vect] \simeq Rep(G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Every such functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibration sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V//G \to \mathbf{B}G
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect_* \to Vect
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Vect
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi ρ
                        %mtd
                          %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V//G &amp;\to&amp; Vect_*
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}G &amp;\stackrel{\rho}{\to}&amp; Vect
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V//G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            on the representation vector space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V := \rho(\bullet)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            is the single object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            on its underlying set, is the fiber of this fibration, so that the action gives rise to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fiber sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V \hookrightarrow V//G \to \mathbf{B}G
                  \\,.
          %p
            As described at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            to the universal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G  \to \mathbf{B}G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on itself,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
